Q: Assigning 128-bit integer in C A: <p>since gcc 14 you can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/language/integer_constant.html#The_type_of_the_integer_constant" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>wb</code> integer suffix</a> to make gcc use exactly enough bits to represent integer<pre><code>//47942806932686753431wb is signed _BitInt(67) literal, uncomment line below to see _BitInt(67) in error message
//void *error = 47942806932686753431wb;


//67 is less bits than 128, gcc sign extends _BitInt(67) to __uint128_t (similar to assigning signed short to unsigned int)
__uint128_t p = 47942806932686753431wb;</code></pre></p>
